.SH "NAME"
sxhkd-indicator \- On\-screen chord\-chain indicator for sxhkd
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fBsxhkd\-indicator\fR \fB\-s\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
sxhkd\-indicator shows a small window while a chord chain of \fBsxhkd\fR is in progress, listing the chords received so far\&. It learns what the daemon is doing from the daemon\(cqs status FIFO (\fBsxhkd \-s\fR \fIPATH\fR) and runs as a separate program, so it can be started, stopped, restyled or replaced without touching the daemon\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
Every option has a short and a long form\&. Long options take their value as \fB\-\-name\fR=\fIVALUE\fR or \fB\-\-name\fR \fIVALUE\fR; \fB\-\-\fR ends the options\&. The program takes no other argument\&.
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Print the help to standard output and exit\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
.RS 4
Print the version information to standard output and exit\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-status\-fifo\fR \fIPATH\fR
.RS 4
Read the status lines from the FIFO at
\fIPATH\fR, the one given to
\fBsxhkd \-s\fR\&. Required\&. The FIFO is created if it does not exist (see the
\fIBehavior\fR
section)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-position\fR \fIPOSITION\fR
.RS 4
Anchor the window at the given
\fIPOSITION\fR
of the screen:
\fItop\fR,
\fItop\-left\fR,
\fItop\-right\fR,
\fIcenter\fR,
\fIcenter\-left\fR,
\fIcenter\-right\fR,
\fIbottom\fR,
\fIbottom\-left\fR
or
\fIbottom\-right\fR
(default
\fItop\-right\fR)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-font\fR \fIFONT\fR
.RS 4
Font of the banner, as a Pango font description (default:
\fImonospace 14\fR)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-foreground\fR \fICOLOR\fR
.RS 4
Foreground color of the banner, as
\fI#rrggbb\fR
or
\fI#rrggbbaa\fR
(default:
\fI#ffffff\fR)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-background\fR \fICOLOR\fR
.RS 4
Background color of the banner, as
\fI#rrggbb\fR
or
\fI#rrggbbaa\fR
(default:
\fI#222222\fR)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
.RS 4
Hide a chain in progress after
\fISECONDS\fR
without a status line, in case the line that ended it was lost\&.
\fISECONDS\fR
is a non\-negative integer;
\fI0\fR
disables this safety timeout (default
\fI3\fR, the daemon\(cqs own default)\&. A locked chain never times out, as in the daemon\&.
.RE
.SH "BEHAVIOR"
.sp
The window lists the chords received so far, separated by semicolons, and ends with a semicolon when the chain is waiting for its next chord or with a colon when the chain is locked\&. It appears when a chain begins (a single\-chord hotkey shows nothing) and disappears when the chain ends, whether because its last chord was received, the abort keysym was pressed or the daemon\(cqs timeout was reached\&. It never takes the keyboard focus, lets pointer events through, and follows changes of the screen size\&.
.sp
\fBsxhkd\-indicator\fR and \fBsxhkd\fR share one policy for the FIFO, so either may start first: whichever finds nothing at \fIPATH\fR creates the FIFO, readable and writable by its owner only, and removes it at exit; the other one finds it and uses it as it is\&. Anything else at the path is an error\&.
.sp
\fBsxhkd\-indicator\fR then waits, without opening the display, until the daemon opens its end of the FIFO\&. Once the daemon is there it connects to the X server, creates its window, and shows the chain in progress as the status lines arrive\&. When the daemon exits, the banner is hidden and \fBsxhkd\-indicator\fR goes back to waiting for the next daemon: it survives a restart of \fBsxhkd\fR\&. \fISIGINT\fR, \fISIGHUP\fR and \fISIGTERM\fR end it at any point, including while it waits\&.
.sp
The FIFO keeps the status lines only until it is full and drops them afterwards, so a line can be lost while the indicator is not reading, for instance while it starts\&. The \fB\-t\fR timeout covers the loss of the line that ended a chain: a chain shown in progress for that long without any line is taken to have ended\&. Lines with a prefix that this version does not know are ignored, as the protocol requires (see the \fIStatus FIFO\fR section of \fBsxhkd\fR(1))\&.
.sp
Translucent colors (an alpha below \fIff\fR) need a compositing manager and an X server that offers a 32\-bit visual\&. Without a compositing manager they render darker and fully transparent areas black; without a 32\-bit visual they are painted fully opaque\&. \fBsxhkd\-indicator\fR warns at startup in either case\&.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.sp
Start the daemon on a FIFO and the indicator on the same one, in either order:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
sxhkd \-s "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sxhkd\&.fifo" &
sxhkd\-indicator \-s "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sxhkd\&.fifo" \-i bottom \-B \*(Aq#222222c0\*(Aq &
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
A second \fB\-s\fR on the daemon feeds another consumer, such as a notification script, without sharing the indicator\(cqs pipe\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.sp
\fBsxhkd\fR(1)
